  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nHot and mostly dry weather is favored this week, but ridging is \nexpected to weaken and leave the door open for low-end isolated rain \nchances (generally around or below 30 percent) across South \nCentral Texas. A weak area of coastal vorticity on Thursday and \nresidual activity/outflow boundaries from troughing or a weak \nfront to the north on Friday are expected to be the main drivers \nof these spotty rain chances, along with recurring opportunities \nfrom the coastal seabreeze. While a lack of wind shear or more \nconcentrated forcing will favor more disorganized activity, the \nhumid air could support a briefly heavier rain.\n\nTemperatures will stay hot and broadly above normal for early/mid \nSeptember. The rain chances will bring scattershot relief from the \nheat for isolated areas, but otherwise highs are expected to remain \nin the upper 90s to low 100s. With dew points remaining elevated, \nheat indices are forecast to continue straddling Heat Advisory \ncriteria at least through the week for areas outside of the Hill \nCountry. The hottest period in the forecast could focus around \nFriday/Saturday when northwesterly flow aloft dislodges hotter air \naloft over the southern Rockies into our area.",
